#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Interactive tool to reconfigure the OneControl/Unity module itself --
writing PIDs over a DIAGNOSTIC session -- as opposed to manage-devices,
which only ever touches this bridge's own D-Bus exposure config
(config.json). Built 2026-08-22 once the underlying mechanisms were all
proven on real hardware: PID 4/5 (identity) writes, PID 161 (dimming vs.
latching behavior), and device_instance-based target disambiguation
(list_unconfigured.py + relay_blip.py's physical confirmation).

Real, physical-effect writes to real hardware, with no config-gated safety
net (an unconfigured device has no device_class/commands_enabled to gate
on -- same as pid_write.py/relay_blip.py). Every run opens with a warning
requiring explicit typed acknowledgment before anything else happens.

Three real operations, matching what's actually been proven -- not a
generic "write any PID" editor (pid_write.py already covers that):

  1) Configure a port -- identity (PID 4/5) and/or any applicable known
     setting (currently just PID 161's dimming-vs-latching behavior,
     can_link.pid_client.KNOWN_SETTINGS), for either a never-configured
     port or an already-configured device. Includes an optional physical
     test step before deciding whether to add a newly-named device to
     config.json.
  2) Unconfigure a port -- reverts identity to FUNCTION_NAME=0/
     FUNCTION_INSTANCE=0.
  3) Back up current settings of every port on the bus -- read-only,
     writes a timestamped snapshot to samples/.

PID 161 is a genuine configuration CHOICE (dimmer vs. plain on/off), not a
bug to fix -- this tool presents it as such in both directions. See
ARCHITECTURE.md's "PID Reconfiguration" design decision.

Unlike manage-devices, this does NOT run off the Cerbo at all: main() opens
a real SocketCanBus and claims a CAN address before any flow runs, so even
the config.json-only parts (e.g. picking an already-configured target) are
unreachable without the real CAN interface. Only the underlying imports
(dbus_bridge.config_manager/device_mapping) are themselves dbus/gi-free,
which is not the same as this tool being usable off-Cerbo.
"""

